# kJubilee — nightly broadcast schedule publish
#
# Install on the app host:
#   cp scripts/kjubilee-schedules.cron /etc/cron.d/kjubilee-schedules
#   chmod 644 /etc/cron.d/kjubilee-schedules
#   systemctl restart cron
#
# ── why 12:00 UTC and not just after midnight ───────────────────────────────
# The engine spec's own scheduler runs at 00:05 UTC, and that is the wrong hour
# for THIS job for two reasons:
#
#   1. Today's schedule has to exist BEFORE today starts. A job that generates
#      the current day at 00:05 leaves five minutes in which every listener
#      resolves against a file that is not there yet.
#   2. Tomorrow's file wants to be at the Cloudflare edge before the rollover,
#      or every client in the world fetches it from the origin within the same
#      minute.
#
# Running at midday with --days 3 solves both: today is already published (and
# republishing it is a no-op, since the generator is deterministic), tomorrow
# goes out twelve hours early and is warm at the edge by midnight, and the day
# after that is a spare. A completely failed run therefore costs nothing —
# there are two more days already published before anyone notices.
#
# ── pools ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# --rebuild-pools is deliberately NOT set. Rebuilding reads the frame headers of
# every track off J:\kjubilee.com\music, which this host cannot see: pools are
# built on the workstation after an ingest and copied to tmp/pools/. A nightly
# job that tried to rebuild them would fail every night on a machine that has no
# music share. See ensurePool() in r2-publish-schedules.js.
#
SHELL=/bin/sh
PATH=/usr/local/sbin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/sbin:/usr/bin:/sbin:/bin
MAILTO=""

0 12 * * * root cd /var/www/kjubilee.com && /usr/bin/node scripts/r2-publish-schedules.js --apply --days 3 >> /var/log/kjubilee-schedules.log 2>&1
